      <article class="project-entry">
        <span class="project-kicker">Swarm Robotics · Optimization</span>
        <h2>Alvik Swarm — Multi-Robot Routing on a Physical Grid</h2>
        <p>
          A fleet of Arduino Alvik rovers turns a classic optimization problem into moving hardware. The
          rovers operate on a printed grid beneath a fixed overhead camera; each carries an AprilTag on
          its top plate, so the moment a powered-on rover enters the camera's view it is registered by
          the central server and appears on the control page. Commands are issued to the whole swarm from
          the server's dashboard.
        </p>
        <p>
          The system localizes each rover by fusing wheel odometry with the overhead camera's AprilTag
          detections — correcting the drift that odometry alone accumulates. On top of that positioning layer, a
          genetic algorithm routes the swarm through a complex variant of the traveling-salesman problem:
          "operators" are dropped off at grid locations and must be picked up again later, and the
          measured objective is the full execution time of the system. The goal is to make the robots
          reflect a real-world routing and logistics optimization problem — algorithm to action.
        </p>
        <div class="project-media">
          <figure class="project-figure">
            <img src="/assets/photos/alvik-tsp.JPEG" alt="Six illuminated Alvik rovers with AprilTags on their top plates navigating a large printed grid on a table in a darkened lab" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>The swarm mid-run: Alvik rovers with AprilTag-topped plates traverse the printed grid while the overhead camera tracks each tag.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="project-entry">
        <span class="project-kicker">Master's Thesis Research</span>
        <h2>Edge-Delegated Authorization for Industrial Robots</h2>
        <p>
          Industrial robots that draw their operating authorization from a cloud identity provider halt
          the moment the network drops — the expiring lease acts as an involuntary kill switch. This
          thesis moves the authorization decision onto edge hardware beside the robot so work can continue
          through an outage. Because no external authority can then revoke permission, the edge node must
          be able to stop the robot itself: it maintains an exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA)
          trust score that decays as local cryptographic verification degrades, and when the score falls
          below threshold it de-energizes a 24&nbsp;V optocoupler wired into the safeguard inputs of a
          Universal Robots UR5 controller.
        </p>
        <p>
          The central contribution is a latency model that predicts, from the verification cycle time and
          the EWMA weight, how quickly that stop occurs — exercised across 335 physical trials on the UR5
          using a profiled elliptic-curve verification workload (~225&nbsp;ms per cycle).
        </p>
        <p class="project-note">
          This is an exploratory investigation into authorization latency. It is <strong>not</strong> a
          validated or standards-compliant functional-safety architecture, and the experimental design
          operates outside the normative frameworks of ISO 13849-1, ISO 13855, and IEC 62061.
        </p>
        <div class="project-media">
          <figure class="project-figure project-figure-portrait">
            <img src="/assets/photos/ur5-wired-in.jpg" alt="Edge node breadboard with lit status LEDs wired into the safeguard input terminals of a Universal Robots UR5 controller cabinet" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>The edge node wired into the UR5 controller's safeguard inputs through the 24&nbsp;V optocoupler stage.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="project-figure">
            <video controls muted playsinline preload="none" poster="/assets/photos/robot-safety-timeline-poster.jpg" width="480" height="270">
              <source src="/assets/photos/robot-safety-timeline.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
            <figcaption>One trial, animated: an injected attack decays the trust score until the threshold crossing halts the robot.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="project-figure">
            <video controls muted playsinline preload="none" poster="/assets/photos/ur5-trial-run-poster.jpg" width="480" height="260">
              <source src="/assets/photos/ur5-trial-run.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
            <figcaption>A physical trial: the UR5 executes its motion program while the edge node's telemetry streams in the terminal overlay.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="project-entry">
        <span class="project-kicker">Smart Mfg &amp; CNC</span>
        <h2>Sensor-Integrated CNC Machining</h2>
        <p>
          A major thread of the lab's work is instrumenting CNC machines with sensors and putting that
          data to work: machine-learning models for predictive tool wear, digital twins of the machining
          process, and data visualization that surfaces overall equipment effectiveness (OEE) for
          optimization and performance improvement. Two machines anchor this work:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Rownd CNC lathe</strong> — A programmable turning center the lab is using to produce
            chess pieces while automating and optimizing the workflow for complex parts that require
            4th-axis milling alongside turning.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Compact metal CNC</strong> — A small metal-cutting mill producing LEGO-scale parts
            with incredibly tight clearances, pushing fixturing and tolerance control at miniature scale.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="project-media">
          <figure class="project-figure">
            <img src="/assets/photos/rownd-wideshot.jpeg" alt="Rownd CNC lathe workstation with its software dashboard on a monitor, the enclosed machine, and a Haas control pendant" loading="lazy" />
